  "body": "> This reminds me a bit of [#69640](https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/69640) for unclaimed bindings on `ng-template`, and [#70808](https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/70808) / [#71844](https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/71844) for class bindings on `ng-container`. In [#70808](https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/70808), which mentioned:\n> \n> > Those are never applied and we should throw at compiler time.\n> \n> The `ng-content` part was also discussed in [#24259](https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/24259) and [#68715](https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/68715), where it was [mentioned](https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/68715#issuecomment-4446935264):\n> \n> > Is this maybe more appropriate as a template diagnostic or eslint? Breaking compilation on something entirely innocuous seems maybe incorrect.\n> \n> I see this proposal covers more cases, but I think some of these checks could also be handled by ESLint.\n> \n> So I think maybe we can comment in that issue or PR\n\nI think the proliferation of agents makes the value of compiler diagnostics higher. Also not everybody uses ESLint and oxlint doesn't even support Angular templates. \nFixing compilation for this case is now as easy as a asking agent to \"fix it\". Every error was silently ignored no-op anyway. This makes it explicit.",
